<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">United Baltic Duchy</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived German client state proclaimed by Baltic German nobles in late 1918, meant to unite Estonia and Livonia under a German duke.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the German occupation of 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic German</a> nobles sought to keep their dominance. Councils dominated by them asked for a union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 5 November 1918 a United Baltic Duchy was proclaimed in Riga, covering the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-estonia">Governorate of Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-livonia">Governorate of Livonia</a>, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia-1918">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> (1918) to join.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-adolf-friedrich-of-mecklenburg">Duke Adolf Friedrich of Mecklenburg</a> was chosen as head of state but never took office. A regency council under Baron Adolf Pilar von Pilchau governed in name only, relying entirely on German troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, who were the great majority of the population, rejected the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> doomed the duchy. The regency council dissolved itself on 28 November 1918, and its territory passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a>, amid fighting with Soviet forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic German</a> volunteers later fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-landeswehr">Landeswehr</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig">